</code></pre> <p>A value of the wrong type raises SQLSTATE <code>22023</code> (invalid_parameter_value); a value out of range raises <code>22003</code> (numeric_value_out_of_range).</p>

Function Differences from jsonb <code>to_cbor(anyelement)</code> See the type mapping <code>cbor_build_array(VARIADIC "any")</code> As <code>jsonb_build_array</code> <code>cbor_build_object(VARIADIC "any")</code> Keys keep their type: an <code>int</code> argument becomes an integer key, a <code>bytea</code> a byte-string key <code>cbor_object(text[])</code>, <code>cbor_object(keys text[], values text[])</code> As <code>jsonb_object</code>: text keys and text values <code>cbor_agg(anyelement)</code>, <code>cbor_agg_strict</code> As <code>jsonb_agg</code>, <code>jsonb_agg_strict</code> <code>cbor_object_agg(key, value)</code>, <code>_strict</code>, <code>_unique</code>, <code>_unique_strict</code> As <code>jsonb_object_agg</code> and variants; keys of any type <pre><code>SELECT cbor_build_object(1, 'one', 'k', '\x00'::bytea, 'n', NULL);
-- {1: "one", "k": h'00', "n": null}

SELECT cbor_object_agg(k, v) FROM (VALUES (1, 'a'), (2, 'b')) x(k, v);
-- {1: "a", 2: "b"}
</code></pre>

Function Differences from jsonb <code>cbor_set(target, path text[], new_value, create_if_missing DEFAULT true)</code> As <code>jsonb_set</code> <code>cbor_set_lax(…, null_value_treatment DEFAULT 'use_json_null')</code> As <code>jsonb_set_lax</code> <code>cbor_insert(target, path text[], new_value, insert_after DEFAULT false)</code> As <code>jsonb_insert</code> <code>cbor_set_path(target, path cbor, new_value, create_if_missing DEFAULT true)</code> <code>cbor_set</code> with a typed path <code>cbor_insert_path(target, path cbor, new_value, insert_after DEFAULT false)</code> <code>cbor_insert</code> with a typed path <code>cbor_delete_text_path(cbor, text[])</code> As <code>jsonb_delete_path</code> (the <code>#-</code> operator) <code>cbor_strip_nulls(target, strip_in_arrays DEFAULT false, strip_undefined DEFAULT false)</code> As <code>jsonb_strip_nulls</code>; <code>undefined</code> is removed too when <code>strip_undefined</code> is true <code>cbor_pretty(cbor)</code> As <code>jsonb_pretty</code>, in EDN <p>A typed path is a CBOR array whose steps keep their type: <code>'["a", 5, h''01'']'</code> is text key <code>"a"</code>, then integer 5 (an array index, or an integer key of a map), then byte-string key <code>h'01'</code>.</p> <pre><code>SELECT cbor_set_path('{"a": {}}', '["a", 5]', '"x"');
-- {"a": {5: "x"}}

SELECT cbor_strip_nulls('{"a": null, "b": undefined, "c": [null]}', strip_in_arrays =&gt; true, strip_undefined =&gt; true);
-- {"c": []}
</code></pre>

Function Differences from jsonb <code>cbor_populate_record(base anyelement, cbor)</code>, <code>cbor_populate_recordset</code> As <code>jsonb_populate_record</code>, <code>_recordset</code> (see below) <code>cbor_populate_record_valid(base anyelement, cbor)</code> As <code>jsonb_populate_record_valid</code>. PostgreSQL 17 and later <code>cbor_to_record(cbor)</code>, <code>cbor_to_recordset(cbor)</code> As <code>jsonb_to_record</code>, <code>_recordset</code> <p>Text keys are matched to column names; keys of other types are ignored. Values convert as in <code>jsonb</code>, plus: byte strings go to <code>bytea</code> columns, and tags 0 and 1 go to <code>timestamptz</code>, tag 37 to <code>uuid</code> and tag 100 to <code>date</code> columns. Other values go through their EDN text into the column type's input function.</p> <pre><code>CREATE TYPE person AS (id int, name text, seen timestamptz);
SELECT * FROM cbor_populate_record(NULL::person, '{"id": 7, "name": "ann", "seen": 1(1700000000), 5: "ignored"}');
-- 7 | ann | 2023-11-14 22:13:20+00
</code></pre>

Condition SQLSTATE Malformed CBOR, invalid EDN, invalid UTF-8, duplicate map keys, invalid bignum <code>22P02</code> invalid_text_representation for text input; <code>22P03</code> invalid_binary_representation for bytes (<code>bytea::cbor</code>, the binary protocol) Depth or expansion limit exceeded <code>54000</code> program_limit_exceeded Cast out of range <code>22003</code> numeric_value_out_of_range Cast from the wrong CBOR type <code>22023</code> invalid_parameter_value Not enough memory for an input-sized buffer <code>53200</code> out_of_memory <pre><code>SELECT repeat('[', 600)::cbor;
-- ERROR:  cbor value rejected: nesting depth exceeds postcbor.max_depth at byte 512
</code></pre>

Kind EDN Notes Integer <code>42</code>, <code>-7</code>, <code>0x2a</code>, <code>0o52</code>, <code>0b101010</code> Any size: beyond 64 bits it is stored as a bignum (tag 2 or 3) Float <code>2.5</code>, <code>1e10</code>, <code>NaN</code>, <code>Infinity</code>, <code>-Infinity</code>, <code>-0.0</code> A number with <code>.</code> or an exponent is a float Text string <code>"text"</code> JSON escapes, including <code>\u</code> surrogate pairs Byte string <code>h'cafe'</code>, <code>b64'AQI'</code>, <code>'abc'</code> Hex (spaces and comments allowed inside), base64 or base64url (padding optional), or the UTF-8 bytes of the text Embedded CBOR <code>&lt;&lt;1, "a"&gt;&gt;</code> A byte string holding the encodings of the items Array <code>[1, 2]</code> Map <code>{"a": 1, 2: "b", h'00': null}</code> Keys of any type Tag <code>1(1700000000)</code>, <code>32("https://example.com")</code> Tag number, then the content in parentheses Simple value <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>simple(16)</code> Date and time <code>dt'2024-01-01T00:00:00Z'</code>, <code>DT'…'</code> RFC 3339 text read as seconds since 1970; <code>DT</code> wraps the number in tag 1 IP address <code>ip'192.0.2.1'</code>, <code>IP'2001:db8::/32'</code> A byte string, or <code>[prefix length, bytes]</code> for a prefix; <code>IP</code> wraps it in tag 52 (IPv4) or 54 (IPv6) <p>Comments are allowed anywhere whitespace is: <code># …</code> to the end of the line, or <code>/ … /</code>. Encoding indicators (<code>_0</code> to <code>_3</code>, <code>_i</code>, and <code>_</code> for indefinite length, as in <code>[_ 1, 2]</code>) are read and ignored, since every value is stored in one form anyway.</p> <pre><code>SELECT '[_ 0x10, 18446744073709551616, h''00 ff'', b64''AQI'', &lt;&lt;1, "a"&gt;&gt;, simple(99)] # comment'::cbor;
-- [16, 2(h'010000000000000000'), h'00ff', h'0102', h'016161', simple(99)]

SELECT 'dt''2024-01-01T00:00:00Z'''::cbor, 'DT''2024-01-01T00:00:00.5Z'''::cbor, 'IP''2001:db8::/32'''::cbor;
-- 1704067200 | 1(1704067200.5) | 54([32, h'20010db8'])
</code></pre> <p>Not accepted, with an error naming the feature: <code>b32''</code> and <code>h32''</code> strings, string concatenation, the <code>...</code> ellipsis, raw strings, hexadecimal floats, other application literals, and trailing commas.</p>
